WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
How models are trained, and why you should care.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:05.016
Modern assistants are built in stages.

3
00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:17.669
Pretraining teaches a model to predict the next token across a huge corpus of text and code; the result is a powerful document-completer with a knowledge cutoff, the date its training data ends.

4
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:29.192
Post-training then shapes it into an assistant: supervised fine-tuning on example conversations, followed by reinforcement learning from human feedback and related methods.

5
00:00:29.472 --> 00:00:43.168
The 2022 InstructGPT paper by Ouyang and colleagues showed how much this stage matters: people preferred outputs from a much smaller instruction-tuned model over those from a far larger base model.

6
00:00:43.448 --> 00:00:54.584
Recent models add reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers, such as math and code, which is a large part of why they reason better than models from a couple of years ago.

7
00:00:54.864 --> 00:00:57.083
Three practical consequences follow.

8
00:00:57.363 --> 00:01:06.259
One: the model doesn’t know anything after its cutoff unless you give it that information, which is the whole motivation for retrieval in Studio 2.

9
00:01:06.539 --> 00:01:14.539
Two: post-training rewards answers that look helpful, so a model may produce a fluent, confident answer when it should say it doesn’t know.

10
00:01:14.819 --> 00:01:17.336
Fluency is not evidence of correctness.

11
00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:23.205
Three: behavior varies between model families and versions, because their post-training differs.

12
00:01:23.485 --> 00:01:32.467
A prompt tuned on one model is a hypothesis on the next, which is why this program treats evaluation as a core skill rather than an afterthought.
